Generic single-database configuration.

Start the PostgreSQL service
sudo service postgresql start
or:
sudo systemctl start postgresql
Create the database and user
Switch to the postgres user:
sudo -u postgres psql
Inside psql, run:
CREATE USER postgres WITH PASSWORD 'postgres';


Switch to the postgres user:
sudo -u postgres psql
Inside psql, run:
CREATE USER postgres WITH PASSWORD 'postgres';
ALTER USER postgres WITH SUPERUSER;
CREATE DATABASE ignition OWNER postgres;
\q
Make sure the server listens on localhost:5432
The default PostgreSQL install usually does this already.


Make sure the server listens on localhost:5432
The default PostgreSQL install usually does this already.
You can verify with:
sudo ss -ltnp | grep 5432
Set the environment variables for the backend
In your shell:

export DB_HOST=localhost
export DB_PORT=5432
export DB_NAME=ignition

export DB_NAME=ignition
export DB_USER=postgres
export DB_PASSWORD=postgres
Or create a file at backend/.env with:

DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
DB_NAME=ignition
DB_USER=postgres
DB_PASSWORD=postgres
Verify the connection
From the backend folder:
cd backend
. .venv/bin/activate

dbname="ignition",
user="postgres",
password="postgres",
host="localhost",
port="5432",
)
print("connected")
conn.close()
PY
If that succeeds, your database is ready.

Next step
Once PostgreSQL is reachable, run:
cd backend
. .venv/bin/activate
alembic revision --autogenerate -m "initial_schema"
alembic upgrade head